* OFTPRCV0 - OFTPD receive rules
*
* Where an incoming file goes. A file that matches no rule is received as
* INPREFIX.<virtual file name>, as it was before rules existed; so a member
* with no rule in it changes nothing.
*
* RULE=name opens a rule (1..8 characters, unique); the keywords after it
* belong to it. The FIRST rule, in the order of this member, that matches
* the partner and the virtual file name decides, so put the narrow rules
* before the wide ones.
*
* FROM     a partner name from OFTPPTN0, or * for any            (*)
* VFN      the virtual file name: * is any number of characters,
*          ? is exactly one; case does not matter                (*)
* DSN      the data set name, with symbols; without it the file
*          gets the INPREFIX name
* REPLACE  Y: an existing data set is replaced once the new file
*          has been received and closed (a file that fails leaves
*          the old data set as it was); N: the file is refused
*          (SFNA 13), the partner may try again                  (N)
* UNIT     VOLSER   where the data set is allocated    (OFTPPRM0's)
* SPACE    tracks, 1-9999; used only when the sender gives no size
* XLATE    Y or N: translate this file or not, instead of what the
*          partner entry says
* JOB      pds(member): a job to submit once the file has been received
*          (the symbols below, ?PFILE ?VFILE ?ID ?KEY, are put into it)
*
* Symbols in DSN= (the data set name is checked when the file arrives; a
* name that comes out invalid refuses the file with SFNA 01 and the
* console says which rule made it):
*   %VFN         the virtual file name
*   %Q1 .. %Q9   its first .. ninth qualifier (between dots)
*   %ID          the partner's name
*   %YYYY %MM %DD %HH %MI %SS    the time the file arrived
*   %CTR         the 4 digits of the sender's file counter
*   %KEY         the spool key, T and 7 digits
* %VFN, %Q1..%Q9 and %CTR carry text the PARTNER chooses into the name, so
* they may NOT stand in the first qualifier (before the first '.'): a rule
* with DSN=%VFN, DSN=PROD%VFN or DSN=%Q1.X would let a partner create or
* replace any data set this started task may write. The member is then not
* used (OFTP010W). Start with a fixed qualifier: DSN=ACCT.IN.%VFN. A DSN=
* that can name a data set under TMPPREFIX is refused as well.
* REPLACE=Y works on a plain sequential data set; a PDS or a VSAM cluster
* is refused (SFNA 12, OFTP234W) and left alone. While a file is
* replaced the old one waits under TMPPREFIX.B and the 7 digits of the spool
* key (OFTP.TMP.B0000012); see docs/installation.md for what to do after a
* crash.
* A qualifier is 1-8 characters and starts with a letter, so a date in
* one needs a letter in front and no more than 7 characters after it:
* Y%YYYY is 5, D%MM%DD is 5, T%HH%MI%SS is 7.
*
* JOB=pds(member): OFTPD reads the member, puts in the symbols
*   ?PFILE  the data set the file was received as
*   ?VFILE  the virtual file name (letters, digits and . - _ @ # $ only)
*   ?ID     the partner          ?KEY  the spool key
* and gives it to its own internal reader. The EERP does not wait for the
* job. A symbol ends at the first character that is no letter or digit:
* ?IDX is another name and stays. A line that grows past column 71 (a //
* line) or 80 is not cut: the job is not submitted, the file is kept
* (OFTP247W).
* Whose job it is: OFTPD sets no USER=. Without one on the job card the job
* does NOT run as the OFTPD started task but as the default user of the
* security product (RAKF: PROD), and has what that user may do under
* your RAKF profiles; what
* that is, only you can say: look it up before you use JOB=. Protect this
* member and the library of the jobs as you protect SYS2.PARMLIB.
* The partner's text must never decide what the job is: put ?VFILE only
* behind a fixed high-level qualifier (DSN=ACCT.IN.?VFILE), never as a whole
* name, as PGM= or in PARM=, not on the job card or in USER=, PASSWORD= or
* GROUP=, and keep VFN= as narrow as the application
* allows (the filter in OFTPD checks the characters, not the meaning:
* SYS1.PARMLIB and IEFBR14 pass it). A partner can have one job queued for
* every file it may send under a matching rule. Partner names used with ?ID
* should be plain names (OFTPPTN0 does not restrict the characters).
*
* F OFTPD,REFRESH reads this member again. A member with any error is not
* used at all: the rules that were in force stay, and the console names
* the line (OFTP010W) and says OFTP025E.
*
* Examples. The rules are tried from the top, so the narrow ORDERS.URGENT
* comes before the ORDERS.* that would match it as well:
*
* RULE=URGENT  FROM=PARTNER1  VFN=ORDERS.URGENT
*              DSN=PROD.ORDERS.URGENT  REPLACE=Y
*              JOB=PROD.JCL(ORDERS)
* RULE=ORDERS  FROM=PARTNER1  VFN=ORDERS.*
*              DSN=PROD.ORDERS.Y%YYYY.D%MM%DD.T%HH%MI%SS
* RULE=INVOICE FROM=*  VFN=INV?????.DATA
*              DSN=ACCT.IN.%ID.%VFN  REPLACE=Y  VOLSER=WORK01  SPACE=30
